- How to reconstruct the exact subarrays efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a concrete DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping subarrays with a fixed per-subarray cost. It requires careful state definitions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling of negative sums.

## Key Concepts

- DP for k disjoint subarrays with end-state tracking
- Prefix sums or running sums to compute subarray value fast
- Reconstruction pointers to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk) (with optimizations)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = NEG;
      if (t > 0) end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How handle cases where all values are negative or c is large?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests a practical DP pattern: partitioning a string into k+1 substrings with a sum objective and exact k cuts, including reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact cuts
- Substring value computation via prefix powers of 10
- Backtracking to recover split points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const a = s.split('').map(Number);
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  const pow10 = new Array(n+1).fill(1);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){pref[i] = pref[i-1]*10 + a[i-1]; pow10[i] = pow10[i-1]*10;}
  const val = (l,r)=> pref[r]-pref[l-1]*pow10[r-l+1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][0] = val(1,i); }
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1]===-Infinity) continue;
        const cur = dp[j][t-1] + val(j+1,i);
        if(cur>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=cur; pre[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct using n and k
  let cuts=[]; let i=n, t=k; while(t>0){ const j=pre[i][t]; cuts.push(j); i=j; t--; }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize for very large n (e.g., n up to 1e5)?
- How to handle large sums (big integers) or modulo arithmetic?
- Could you adapt for non-contiguous partitions with penalties?

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (block maximum). It extends beyond standard max-sum variants and evaluates careful indexing, prefix handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- **Contiguous partition DP**: State dp[i][t] represents the minimum cost for the prefix of length i using exactly t blocks
- **Transition**: Computes the running maximum for the last block while finding the optimal split point
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking through the DP table to determine block endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumOfBlockMax(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const split = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      let maxVal = 0;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= t - 1; j--) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
        const cost = dp[j][t - 1] + maxVal;
        if (cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cost;
          split[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = split[i][t];
    blocks.unshift({ start: j + 1, end: i });
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests partitioning a sequence into exactly k blocks where block value is non-linear in length. It requires a careful DP with reconstruction and shows understanding of prefix sums and state transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Non-linear block value and reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysProduct(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + a[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pick = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j-1][t-1] + (i - j + 1) * (pref[i] - pref[j-1]);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          pick[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

